INT. BAKERY WALK-IN COOLER - BEFORE DAWN

Steel shelves of dough trays. Breath fogs. A bare bulb swings slightly from its cord.

CASIMIR LEWANDOWSKI, sixty, the head baker, in a white coat, stands at the back wall. His hands shake. Across from him ROXANE DELACROIX-HUNT, forty, in a black wool coat and leather gloves, holds a ledger.

He looks at the door. She looks at him. She sets the ledger on a tray of dough, and the dough sighs under it.

CASIMIR
You said Tuesday.

ROXANE
It is Tuesday.

CASIMIR
It's four in the morning.

ROXANE
Then it's early Tuesday.

She opens the ledger. He shuts his eyes. She turns a page and runs a gloved finger down a column.

CASIMIR
Forty years, I never cooked the books.

ROXANE
You cooked them for thirty-nine.

He opens his eyes. He reaches into his coat and she watches him do it, patient as a cat. He draws out a small brass key.

CASIMIR
It's in the flour bin.

ROXANE
All of it?

CASIMIR
All of it.

She takes the key. She does not move toward the door. Instead she closes the ledger and sets it back in his hands.

ROXANE
Keep this. It's the only copy.

He stares. She straightens her gloves. She walks out, and the heavy door swings shut behind her, and the bulb sways, and he stands among the dough with the book clutched to his chest like a hymnal.
